<p>ARB initial cost is higher but lifetime maintenance cost is significantly lower. See <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="design-heading">Design Considerations</h2>
<h3>Belt Width &amp; Length</h3>
<p>ARB belt width must accommodate the widest product plus the divert path. The product must travel diagonally from its starting position to the takeaway point within the belt length. Wider belts and steeper divert angles (45 degrees) require more belt length for the product to clear the belt edge. Intralox provides layout engineering tools that calculate the required belt dimensions for specific product sizes and divert angles.</p>
<h3>Product Bottom Surface</h3>
<p>ARB rollers engage the product bottom surface to generate lateral force. Products must have a relatively flat, rigid bottom. Soft-bottom products (like poly bags with loose fill) may deform around the rollers instead of being pushed. Very smooth, hard bottoms (like glass) work well. Very rough or uneven bottoms may not engage the rollers consistently. Intralox recommends product testing for unfamiliar product types.</p>
<h3>Activation Zone Control</h3>
<p>Activation zones can be fixed (always active) or switchable (activated by pneumatic or electric actuators). Fixed zones suit applications where every product diverts (like a dedicated lane split). Switchable zones suit sorting applications where only selected products divert based on barcode scans or other criteria. Switchable activation adds complexity and cost but provides selective diverting capability.</p>
<h3>Speed &amp; Throughput</h3>
<p>ARB conveyor speeds typically range from 150 to 400+ FPM depending on product size and divert angle. Higher belt speeds increase throughput but reduce the time available for the roller force to complete the divert. Larger, heavier products require more time (slower belt speed or longer activation zone) to complete the divert. Intralox publishes throughput specifications for standard product categories.</p>
<h3>Integration with Existing Systems</h3>
<p>ARB conveyors integrate with upstream barcode scanners, vision systems, and conveyor controls for intelligent sorting. They interface with downstream <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>, <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a>, chutes, and <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation zones</a>. Intralox and its integration partners (system integrators like Dematic, Intelligrated, and Bastian Solutions) design complete ARB sorting systems with controls and software.</p>
<h2 id="industries-heading">Industry Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Parcel distribution:</strong> ARB is widely deployed in parcel carrier sort centers (UPS, FedEx, DHL, national post services). The technology handles the enormous variety of parcel sizes, shapes, and weights that flow through distribution hubs. ARB singulation converts randomly loaded induction streams into scannable single-file flows. ARB diverting routes packages to destination chutes at rates exceeding 60 packages per minute per divert point.</p>
<p><strong>E-commerce fulfillment:</strong> E-commerce warehouses use ARB to sort outbound orders to shipping lanes by carrier, service level, or geographic zone. The mix of polybags, padded mailers, small boxes, and large cases that characterizes e-commerce order streams suits ARB's ability to handle diverse product sizes without adjustment.</p>
<p><strong>Food and beverage packaging:</strong> ARB conveyors sort cases of food and beverage products to palletizing lanes by SKU, flavor, or size. The gentle divert action prevents case damage that mechanical pushers cause. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food-grade</a> ARB belt materials meet FDA requirements for incidental contact.</p>
<p><strong>Retail distribution:</strong> Retail distribution centers use ARB to sort cases and cartons to store-specific lanes. A single ARB sorter replaces multiple mechanical diverters along a trunk line, reducing equipment count, maintenance, and floor space.</p>
<p><strong>Airport baggage handling:</strong> Airport baggage systems use ARB for bag sorting and diverting to makeup carousels. The gentle handling reduces bag damage claims. The no-gap capability handles the dense bag flows that occur during peak travel periods.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is an activated roller belt (ARB)?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A modular belt with embedded angled rollers that spin on demand to divert products diagonally. Developed by Intralox. The rollers activate when they pass over raised pads beneath the belt. No mechanical diverters, pusher plates, or pop-up wheels above the belt surface. Handles diverting, singulation, alignment, and merge.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How does ARB technology work?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Small rollers at 30 or 45 degrees are molded into belt modules. When a module passes over an activation pad beneath the belt, friction spins the roller, pushing the product diagonally. Products not over active zones travel straight. Activation zones can be fixed (always on) or switchable (selective diverting).</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">ARB advantages over traditional diverters?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>No moving mechanical parts above the belt. No product gap requirements. Multiple divert points per belt. Gentle handling (no impact). Very low maintenance. Higher reliability. Handles mixed product sizes without adjustment. Trade-off: higher initial belt cost than simple mechanical diverters.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What products can ARB handle?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Flat-bottom products: cases, cartons, trays, totes, polybags, mailers, parcels. Sizes from 6 inches to 36+ inches. Handles wide product variety without changeover. Products must have a flat, relatively rigid bottom for roller engagement. Very heavy products (over 100 lbs) or soft-bottom items may not divert reliably.</p>

</div>
